File Documentation

Project: Satellite Constellation Fault Simulator (Basilisk)
Version: 1.0
Last Updated: 31 October 2025
Basilisk Version: 1.18.0
Platform: Windows 10 or later
Python Version: 3.10 or later

Core Module Files
BSK_masters.py

Purpose:
This module was originally designed as a master orchestrator for early Basilisk simulations. It defines the base classes that initialize and run a Basilisk environment and manage scenario execution.

Details:

Provides two core base classes: BSKSim and BSKScenario.

BSKSim sets up the Basilisk simulation base class and initializes system components.

BSKScenario manages the simulation configuration, time steps, and data recording.

Although the current project uses spacecraft_simulation.py as the main engine, several legacy fault modules still reference this file for backward compatibility.

spacecraft_simulation.py

Purpose:
This is the main simulation engine that connects the graphical user interface (GUI) with the Basilisk simulation framework. It is responsible for creating satellites, managing faults, running the simulation, and generating outputs.

Details:

Builds and configures spacecraft objects, clusters, and orbital environments.

Sets up orbital dynamics, gravity models, attitude control, and communication links.

Manages the timing and injection of faults across satellites.

Collects telemetry data such as power, reaction wheel speeds, and attitude control errors.

Generates plots and exports visualization data for 3D playback in Vizard.

Uses a modular configuration structure called SimulationConfig that contains duration, logging, and output settings.

Supports multiple satellites and formations such as line, triangle, and diamond configurations.

Coordinates with the fault_loader.py, plots.py, and cluster_integration.py modules to ensure simulation accuracy and synchronization.

spacecraft_simulator_gui.py

Purpose:
This module is the main Tkinter-based graphical interface for the simulator. It serves as the central control hub that allows users to configure constellations, inject faults, run simulations, and view results.

Details:

Manages all GUI tabs through a notebook interface.

Handles user inputs, event callbacks, and status updates.

Executes simulations in a separate background thread to avoid freezing the interface.

Provides options to open folders, save configurations, and launch external tools such as Vizard.

Uses rw_fault_help.py for context-sensitive help messages.

Coordinates data sharing among all GUI tabs through a shared data dictionary.

Automatically manages directories for logs, plots, and visualization exports.

Provides error handling and dependency checks for Basilisk and TensorFlow modules.

fault_loader.py

Purpose:
This module dynamically loads and executes fault definitions from the faults/ directory. It allows faults to be added or modified without changing the main codebase.

Details:

Scans the faults/ directory at runtime and identifies all available fault modules.

Provides a consistent interface for running faults and retrieving their data.

Contains functions such as get_fault_scenario_class() and run_scenario() to simplify module access.

Automatically extracts simulation data for post-processing and visualization.

Supports legacy modules for backward compatibility and uses a plugin-style architecture.

Handles missing modules gracefully and provides warnings rather than errors.

plots.py

Purpose:
The plots.py file is the centralized plotting module that handles all visual outputs of the simulator. It produces both scientific and operational plots related to system performance.

Details:

Creates plots for attitude control, power systems, fault progression, and cluster communication.

Uses telemetry data collected during the simulation to produce high-quality figures.

Supports both single-satellite and multi-satellite visualization.

Uses Matplotlib’s “Agg” backend for reliable non-interactive rendering.

Organizes and saves plots into specific subdirectories under plots/.

Provides fallback options that generate synthetic plots if real data are unavailable.

Integration and Utility Modules
formation_geometry.py

Defines orbital geometries and relative positioning patterns for satellite formations. It supports line, triangle, diamond, and circular configurations. The module evenly distributes satellites in orbit and calculates position vectors based on altitude, inclination, and right ascension.

cluster_integration.py

Handles multi-satellite cluster creation and leader–follower management. It assigns leader and follower satellites, synchronizes their orbits, and enables coordinated fault injection and recovery. The module also supports integration with communication visualization tools.

communication_visualization.py

Visualizes inter-satellite communication links and network activity. It displays leader–follower communication lines, data throughput, latency, and link status. The module provides options to save communication data for later analysis or visual playback.

message_data.py and satellite_definition.py

Contain data structures and helper functions used to store satellite properties, message logs, and simulation parameters. These files define how satellites and communication messages are represented internally during the simulation.

run_fault_simulator.py

The entry point of the application. It initializes environment variables, checks dependencies, and starts the GUI application. It ensures that Basilisk is correctly installed and that required directories such as logs/, plots/, and Vizfile/_VizFiles/ exist.

rw_fault_help.py

Stores textual help content for each GUI tab and fault type. It serves as a local, offline help reference system that can display context-specific explanations, troubleshooting information, and usage instructions directly within the GUI.

style.py

Defines the visual appearance of the GUI. It sets up fonts, colors, and widget styles to maintain a consistent interface design. It also adapts the GUI layout to different screen resolutions and ensures that the application follows a simple, readable theme.

Fault Module Files (faults/)

Each module in the faults/ directory defines a different type of system failure that can be injected into a satellite during simulation.

friction_fault.py

Simulates the effects of increased friction in reaction wheel bearings.

Models additional torque loss and heat generation.

Tracks cumulative energy losses and performance degradation.

Produces wheel speed, temperature, and power consumption plots.

powerlimit_fault.py

Models electrical or mechanical power limitations.

Reduces maximum torque output and simulates power brownouts.

Enables secondary wheel activation to compensate for lost performance.

Outputs power consumption and efficiency analysis plots.

encoder_fault.py

Simulates sensor bias and noise in encoder readings.

Adds bias to angular velocity measurements and random noise to simulate faulty sensors.

Results in attitude control instability and increased control effort.

Produces attitude error and control torque plots.

battery_fault.py

Represents battery degradation or power system failure.

Models reduced capacity, increased discharge rate, and safe mode transitions.

Integrates with the SimpleBattery model in Basilisk.

Outputs battery discharge, power balance, and safe-mode timing plots.

rw_fault.py

Provides backward compatibility for older reaction wheel fault models.

Acts as a wrapper for updated modules.

Maintains compatibility with legacy scripts and external automation.

Fault Detection and XAI Modules
fault_detection_router.py

Acts as a dispatcher for the different fault detection methods available in the simulator. It receives data from the simulation and passes it to either the Isolation Forest or Neural Network-based model depending on the selected method.

isolation_forest_fault_detection.py

Implements an Isolation Forest algorithm from scikit-learn to detect anomalies in satellite telemetry. It works on real-time data and provides anomaly scores and confidence levels.

real_ml_fault_detection.py

Implements a deep learning-based autoencoder that identifies anomalies by reconstructing input telemetry and measuring reconstruction errors. The pre-trained model is stored in anomaly_detection_model.keras.

satellite_xai.py

Provides Explainable AI support using SHAP and LIME libraries. It explains why specific anomalies were detected and highlights which telemetry features contributed most to the decision. All generated explanations are saved as visual reports in core/xai_plots/.

DRL Integration Modules
drl_integration_bridge.py

Connects the Basilisk simulation engine with Deep Reinforcement Learning models. It prepares the simulation state for input to the DRL model, executes the chosen policy, and applies the resulting actions to the simulation.

drl_integration_helper.py

Contains utility functions that normalize data, manage training states, and process feedback from the DRL environment.

drl_model_manager.py

Handles the loading, saving, and selection of DRL models. It manages models for PPO, DQN, and TDHD algorithms and provides versioning and checkpoint capabilities.

drl_config.py

Stores configuration settings for DRL runs, including learning rates, exploration parameters, and episode lengths. It provides a structured configuration interface for the GUI.

simple_task_drl.py

Implements a lightweight DRL test environment that can be used for isolated debugging or simplified experiments separate from the full Basilisk simulation.

GUI Tab Modules (core/gui_tab/)
base_tab.py

Defines shared methods and templates for all GUI tabs. It provides utility functions for message handling, logging, and help access. Every other tab inherits from this base class to maintain consistent functionality and design.

constellation_tab.py

Allows users to design satellite constellations.

Supports adding, removing, and editing satellites and clusters.

Provides predefined formations such as line, triangle, and diamond.

Allows users to configure orbit parameters such as altitude and inclination.

Performs validation on input values and shows a preview of satellite positions.

target_tab.py

Enables users to define ground targets and assign satellites for observation.

Displays a map view for visual reference.

Calculates coverage periods and target visibility windows.

Supports automatic assignment of satellites to targets.

fault_tab.py

Provides an interface for injecting faults.

Lists all available fault types from the faults/ directory.

Allows configuration of fault magnitude, start time, and duration.

Displays a summary of all active and scheduled faults.

fault_detection_tab.py

Configures fault detection algorithms and thresholds.

Allows switching between Isolation Forest and Neural Autoencoder detectors.

Displays anomaly scores and detected faults.

Provides options to generate XAI explanations for detected anomalies.

communication_tab.py

Displays real-time or post-simulation communication between satellites.

Shows leader–follower network links and latency information.

Visualizes communication health through color-coded lines.

task_reassignment_tab.py

Integrates Deep Reinforcement Learning-based fault recovery.

Runs trained DRL policies (PPO, DQN, TDHD).

Reassigns tasks to healthy satellites after a fault.

Displays DRL performance metrics such as reward curves and reassignment summaries.

drl_tab.py

Provides controls for DRL model management and configuration.

Allows training, testing, and loading of pre-trained models.

Displays loss, reward, and performance metrics.

Connects directly to the DRL modules and bridges with the simulation engine.

visualization_tab.py

Allows configuration of orbit and camera visualization options.

Provides camera presets, orbit colors, and label controls.

Generates live previews using Matplotlib.

Exports visualization settings for Vizard playback.

Note: The GUI does not render live 3D; 3D visualization is handled externally through Vizard.

output_tab.py

Manages output settings for the simulation.

Sets simulation duration (in minutes).

Allows users to enable or disable specific outputs such as plots, logs, or binary files.

Provides progress monitoring and real-time log updates.

results_tab.py

Displays simulation results after execution.

Automatically detects generated plots and displays them in an embedded viewer.

Allows users to export plots and logs to external directories.

Provides shortcuts to open folders for quick access to outputs.

DRL Package (DRL/)

The DRL folder contains algorithms and training utilities used for intelligent fault recovery and task reassignment.

PPO.py

Implements the Proximal Policy Optimization algorithm, which is the primary DRL method used in this project. It is suited for continuous control and has been trained for task reassignment.

DQNYear2.py

Implements a Deep Q-Network approach for discrete decision-making. It is an alternative to PPO and used in simpler fault recovery scenarios.

TDHDYear2.py

Implements a Twin Delayed Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (TD3) algorithm with hindsight replay. It is an experimental model used for complex control problems.

Envs.py and Tools.py

Define the reinforcement learning environment and provide utility functions such as experience replay buffers, normalization, and plotting tools.

integration.py and integration_faults.py

Handle communication between the Basilisk environment and the DRL algorithms. These modules manage the data flow from simulation to DRL agent and back.

drl_training_wrapper.py

Provides a high-level interface for running DRL training sessions directly from the GUI.

patch_bsk_rl.py

Contains compatibility fixes that allow Basilisk to communicate with the DRL modules smoothly.

Model Files (models/)

The models/ directory includes Basilisk-specific models for spacecraft dynamics and flight software.

BSK_Dynamics.py: Defines spacecraft physical parameters, mass properties, and sensor configurations.

BSK_Fsw.py: Implements flight software logic including attitude control and reaction wheel management.

BSK_MultiSatDynamics.py / BSK_MultiSatFsw.py: Extend the single-satellite models to multiple satellites operating as a cluster.

BSK_FormationDynamics.py / BSK_FormationFsw.py: Implement control algorithms and dynamics specific to formation flying.

model_registry.json: Contains metadata used to identify available model configurations.

Contributing
Adding New Fault Types

Create a new file in the faults/ directory.

Inherit from the appropriate base class (e.g., BSKScenario).

Implement methods for initialization, fault injection, and output generation.

Ensure that the new fault is automatically recognized by fault_loader.py.

Extending the GUI

Create a new tab class that inherits from BaseTab.

Define its interface and controls in create_tab_ui().

Add the new tab to the GUI manager in spacecraft_simulator_gui.py.

Update rw_fault_help.py to include help content for the new feature.

